        'details.volcano.preview_alt': 'Screenshot of the volcano scene',
        'details.volcano.text_1': 'The volcano is modelled in simplified form as a lathe, a profile rotated around its axis. The lava, in the form of metaballs, flows down a slightly inclined straight line.',
        'details.volcano.scroll_hint': 'About the volcano as a lathe and the lava flow along a straight line',
        'details.volcano.description.lathe_heading': 'The volcano as a solid of revolution',
        'details.volcano.description.lathe_text_1': 'The lava landscape needs a volcano. Instead of modelling it point by point, we only draw its outline with three points: foot, crater rim and crater floor. This line is turned once around the vertical axis. The result is a cone with a crater. In technical terms, this technique is called a lathe.',
        'details.volcano.description.profile_axis': 'Axis of rotation',
        'details.volcano.description.profile_foot': 'Foot',
        'details.volcano.description.profile_rim': 'Crater rim',
        'details.volcano.description.profile_crater': 'Crater floor',
        'details.volcano.description.lathe_figure_alt': 'A vertical axis with a curved arrow around it. To the right of it an orange line through three points: foot, crater rim and crater floor. To the left the same line mirrored and dashed, plus two dashed ellipses at the height of the foot and of the crater rim.',
        'details.volcano.description.lathe_figure_caption': 'The profile (solid) is turned around the axis of rotation. Dashed: the mirrored side and the circles that the foot and the crater rim trace while turning.',
        'details.volcano.description.flow_heading': 'Lava flow on a straight line',
        'details.volcano.description.flow_text_1': 'The lava is made of metaballs. Here, however, they do not move freely: each ball starts above the centre of the crater and glides outwards at a constant speed. Its path is a straight line with the familiar equation:',
        'details.volcano.description.flow_text_2': 'Here x is the distance from the centre, b the spawn height and m the slope. Because the lava flows downwards, m is negative.',
        'details.volcano.description.flow_ground': 'Ground',
        'details.volcano.description.flow_figure_alt': 'Side view: the volcano in section and an orange example line that starts at the height b above the crater, lies just above the slope at first and then dips into the volcano. Further down there is a dashed ball whose centre lies on the line inside the volcano and whose upper part sticks out of the slope. Below the ground the line continues dashed.',
        'details.volcano.description.flow_figure_caption': 'Side view with an example line: the path of the metaball starts at the height b, lies just above the slope at first and dips deeper and deeper into the volcano. Dashed: a ball whose upper part still sticks out of the slope, and the hidden path below the ground.',
        'details.volcano.description.flow_text_3': 'The line of the metaballs does not have the same slope as the volcano. Where the metaball runs inside the volcano, only the upper part of the balls sticks out of the slope, and it looks as if the lava flowed down the surface and became less along the way.',
